{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050021 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161771 :question/math-academy-id "e-8156" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad R=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P=P^T.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q=Q^T.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since $r_{12} \\neq r_{21},$ we obtain $R \\neq R^T.$\n\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & {\\color{red}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-2}} & -1 \\\\ {\\color{red}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{0}} & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162586 :question/math-academy-id "q-93583" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162613 :question/math-academy-id "q-93994" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{12}\\ne {q}_{21},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272114 :question/math-academy-id "q-140504" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P,$ $Q,$ and $R.$\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275133 :question/math-academy-id "q-143679" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the matrices are symmetric.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275134 :question/math-academy-id "q-143680" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 11 & -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357611 :question/math-academy-id "q-93985" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357612 :question/math-academy-id "q-93988" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2} & 7 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{12}\\ne {q}_{21},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357613 :question/math-academy-id "q-93993" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\sqrt{5} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366553 :question/math-academy-id "q-93987" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0.5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0.5 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other 